(a) The county E-911 advisory board overseeing authority will be the county board of commissioners.
(b) The county E-911 advisory board voting members will consist of the following: the county E-911 director or appointee; the county sheriff or appointee; the county Fire/EMS chief or appointee; the county EMA director or appointee; the county planning commission director or appointee; the Homer fire chief or appointee; the Baldwin fire chief or appointee; the Baldwin chief of police or appointee; the Maysville chief of police or appointee; Maysville fire chief or appointee; the department of natural resources law enforcement official; and a state forestry area ranger or appointee.
(c) A quorum of the advisory board shall be a majority of the membership. No business shall be conducted by the advisory board unless the quorum is present.
(d) Guest speakers will be permitted to attend if proper notice is given and the topic directly relates to an emergency system or need. No guest will be allowed to vote on any topic.
(e) The advisory board shall hold regular quarterly meetings at 2:00 p.m. on the second Monday of every third month following October 8, 2001. The meetings will be held at the county fire administrative offices training room. The meetings will not be held unless the quorum is present and quarterly meeting dates may be changed by a vote from the quorum. Special called meetings may be necessary from time to time and may be called by the chairman or a majority of the board. All recommendations shall be sent directly to the county board of commissioner's and county E-911 director.
(f) On the first meeting of the county E-911 advisory board the members will elect a chairman for the meetings and a secretary.
(g) The duties of the advisory board include, but are not limited to the following:
(1) Review and analyze the progress by public safety agencies in the continued development of the 911 and central communications systems;
(2) Recommend steps of action to affect the necessary coordination, regulation and development of the 911 system;
(3) Identify and/or review mutual aid agreements necessary to the operation of the 911 system;
(4) Assist in the promulgation of necessary rules, regulations, and operating procedures necessary to notify various agencies that an emergency exists and ensure proper response, and any other such policy as shall be deemed necessary and appropriate;
(5) Act as a quality assurance board in the management and operation of the central dispatch center;
(6) Make recommendations concerning equipment and coordinate the acquisition of appropriate radio equipment for all users to ensure compatibility with the system as a whole.
(Res. of 10-5-2001)

